<launch>

     <arg name="launch_only_arm" default="false" />

          <let name="urdf_path" 
               value="$(find-pkg-share my_robot_description)/urdf/standalone_arm.urdf.xacro" 
               if="$(var launch_only_arm)"/>
               <let name="urdf_path" 
               value="$(find-pkg-share my_robot_description)/urdf/my_robot.urdf.xacro"
               unless="$(var launch_only_arm)" />
          <let name="rviz_config_path" 
               value="$(find-pkg-share my_robot_description)/rviz/arm_config.rviz"
               if="$(var launch_only_arm)" />
               <let name="rviz_config_path" 
               value="$(find-pkg-share my_robot_description)/rviz/urdf_config_far_view.rviz"
               unless="$(var launch_only_arm)" />

     <node pkg="robot_state_publisher" exec="robot_state_publisher">
          <param name="robot_description"
                    type="str"
                    value="$(command 'xacro $(var urdf_path)')" />   <!-- 'xacro $(var "variable name")' -->
     </node>

     <!-- These packages are accessable to us because in the bashrc we source /opt/ros/jazzy -->
     <node pkg="joint_state_publisher_gui" exec="joint_state_publisher_gui" />

     <!-- If I am to use the variable for the path rviz_config_path, I must do so with 
          $(var rviz_config_path), not rviz_config_path, instead of that long thing/path -->
     <!-- Edit, that is actually a far better approach because if I want to change the urdf or rviz path
          then I can do it by changing the variable instead of scrolling down and searching for the path
          manually -->
     <node pkg="rviz2" exec="rviz2" output="screen" 
               args="-d $(var rviz_config_path)" />

</launch>

<!--
  USEFUL NOTES/ORIENTING

    Context, here I was to start the pkg's robot_state_publisher and joint_state_publisher_gui.
    I didn't know how I could myself figure out which pkg's to start and how I could start them
    in the first place when they weren't in my workspace.

    The answer the those two questions is that the packages ros sees comes from the sourcing.
    In bashrc we source several directories, specifically /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash and 
    ~/ros2_ws/install/setup.bash. This double sourcing is called an underlay + overlay model.
    The latest source is the overlay, and if any package name exists on both layers, then the
    overlay pkg overrides the underlay pkg.

    One must know which packages/nodes to start beforehand. One can list all pkg's ros sees with
    "ros2 pkg list"

    ### "ros2 pkg list | grep <string>" finds the pkg's ros sees that has <string> in their name

    ### "ros2 pkg executables <pkg_name>" gives all executables in <pkg_name>, if no <pkg_name> is 
        provided then all "package executable" pairs are displayed
    
    ### "ros2 pkg prefix <pkg_name>" gives where ros found the package <pkg_name>, so it will give a path

    ### "echo $AMENT_PREFIX_PATH" gives the overlay -> underlay situation in the shell,
                                  so something like /home/mathias/ros2_ws/install/my_package:/opt/ros/jazzy
                                                                                     overlay:underlay

    The <arg name="launch_only_arm" default="0" /> makes if-else logic, here if-unless
    AND it makes so it can be changed at launch from the terminal by writing
    ros2 launch my_robot_description display.launch.xml launch_only_arm:=true (or false)

-->